      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'd be happy to help summarize the main moral arguments for both sides. \n\nReligious conservatives typically argue that physician-assisted suicide is morally equivalent to murder and grounds a slippery slope leading to the devaluation of human life, citing Judeo-Christian values emphasizing the sanctity of life and the value of each person's existence. \n\nIn contrast, religious liberals tend to frame physician-assisted suicide as a compassionate response to suffering and terminal illness, relying on principles of non-maleficence (do no harm), beneficence (do good), and autonomy (respecting a person's capacity to make decisions).",
